## 📌 Problem Statement

Improper waste segregation is a major challenge for effective recycling. People often have difficulty identifying whether an item belongs to categories such as plastic, paper, glass, metal, cardboard, or general trash.

EcoVision is an AI-powered computer vision application that automatically classifies waste images and provides a simple recycling recommendation.

The goal of the project is to demonstrate how Machine Learning and computer vision can be applied to support better waste segregation and recycling awareness.

---

## 🎯 Project Objective

The objective of EcoVision is to:

* Automatically classify waste from an image
* Identify the waste category
* Display the model's confidence
* Provide a recycling recommendation
* Make the trained model accessible through a simple web application

---

## 📊 Dataset

The project uses a labeled waste image dataset containing six categories:

* Cardboard
* Glass
* Metal
* Paper
* Plastic
* Trash

The images were organized into class-specific folders and processed for image classification.

---

## 🔄 Approach

The project follows a complete Machine Learning workflow.

### 1. Data Collection

A publicly available waste image dataset was used for training and evaluation.

### 2. Data Exploration

The dataset was analyzed to:

* Count images per category
* Check class distribution
* Visualize sample images
* Understand the characteristics of the dataset

### 3. Image Preprocessing

Images were resized to `224 × 224` pixels.

Data augmentation techniques were applied to improve model generalization.

### 4. Model Development

Two different models were trained and evaluated:

* Custom CNN
* MobileNetV2 Transfer Learning

### 5. Model Evaluation

Models were compared using validation accuracy and classification performance.

### 6. Model Selection

The model with the strongest validation performance was selected as the final model.

### 7. Deployment

The final model was integrated into a Streamlit web application and deployed publicly.

---

## 🤖 Models

### Custom CNN

A convolutional neural network was developed using:

* Convolutional layers
* Max pooling
* Dense layers
* Dropout
* Softmax output

### MobileNetV2

MobileNetV2 with ImageNet pretrained weights was also evaluated using transfer learning.

The pretrained feature extractor was combined with a custom classification layer for the six waste categories.

